The interpreter says why it learned a block (FEX 3bec2ac, on top of 0030)

"[interp] learned block A: not in the AOT image" said the same whether no
loaded code cache had the block or one had it and an invalidation dropped
it since. NFSU2 on the iPad interprets 64 blocks of SPEED2.EXE that its
code map has, and which of the two it is decides the fix. As [aot-miss]
does for a block compiled at run time, the line now names the reason:
"a code cache had it, and it was dropped since (see [code-inval])" or
"not in the AOT image".

diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Core.cpp b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Core.cpp
index 02e5ec1..154ac8f 100644
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Core.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Core.cpp
@@ -2399,7 +2399,11 @@ ContextImpl::GenerateInterpreterIR(FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState* Thread, u
     }
     if (New) {
       if (Count <= 64) {
-        LogMan::Msg::EFmt("[interp] learned block {:#x}: not in the AOT image, interpreted", GuestRIP);
+        // WinPad: as [aot-miss], whether a loaded code cache had the block (and an invalidation
+        // dropped it) or none has it.
+        LogMan::Msg::EFmt("[interp] learned block {:#x}: {}, interpreted", GuestRIP,
+                          CodeCache.WasLoadedFromCache(GuestRIP) ? "a code cache had it, and it was dropped since (see [code-inval])" :
+                                                                   "not in the AOT image");
       }
       if (CodeMapWriter) {
         auto Region = SyscallHandler->LookupExecutableFileSection(Thread, GuestRIP);
